# NOTICE — source data, attribution and terms

Every task in this repository is derived from a source dataset (or, for the synthetic
family, from a closed-form objective with no dataset at all). The table below names the
source behind each family present in the **public half**, the property that is ranked, and
the terms under which the source is available. Attribution for a CC BY 4.0 source is owed
to its authors, not to us.

The MOF family, whose source publishes no license at all, is entirely in the private half
and no part of it is redistributed here.

| Family | Tasks | Source | Property ranked | Terms |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| NLO | 12 | NOEMD (Xie et al. 2023) | max abs d_ij (pm/V), refractive-index anisotropy | **by permission** — see below |
| B3-real | 9 | UCI superconductivity (Hamidieh 2018); NIMS fatigue (Agrawal et al. 2014) | critical temperature (K); fatigue strength (MPa) | CC BY 4.0; public (NIMS) |
| Crystal-DFT | 6 | JARVIS `dft_3d` (Choudhary et al. 2020), pinned at 75,993 entries | piezoelectric d_ij, spin-orbit spillage | as published (NIST) |
| Synthetic | 6 | none — analytic objective over a fictional alloy schema | fictional alloy response | n/a |
| htsteel | 4 | author-compiled high-temperature steel database | yield strength, elongation, toughness, creep rupture | **derived tasks released; the compilation is not** |
| Perovskite | 4 | The Perovskite Database Project (Jacobsson et al. 2022), Zenodo 5837035 | power-conversion efficiency (%abs), retention | CC BY 4.0 |
| Thermoelectrics | 4 | Starrydata (Katsura et al. 2025) | zT, power factor, thermal conductivity | CC BY 4.0 |
| Olympus | 1 | Olympus (Häse et al. 2021) | Suzuki coupling yield (%) | as published |

## Two sources that need saying plainly

**NLO — released by permission, not by license.** The structure–property records behind the
twelve `nlo_*` tasks carry no open license. They are released here with the written
permission of the author of NOEMD, who was told exactly what would go out before anything was
published: the 300 unique structures those tasks draw on, with their labels, source
identifiers stripped, and the source cited wherever the family is named. **The permission
covers that subset and not the database.** A recipient inherits that situation rather than a
license, and should obtain the source under its own terms for anything beyond this subset.

**htsteel — the tasks are released, the compilation is not.** The four `b3_htsteel_*` tasks
here are derived from a high-temperature steel database compiled by one of the authors and not
distributable as a whole. The derived task files (training tables, candidate pools, answer
keys) are released; the underlying compilation is not.

